          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The concept of a
            %strong Yoneda structure
            provides in a general 2-categorical setting the axiomatic description of the formal properties of the usual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} presheaf construction
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"}> locally
            )
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small categories
          %h2#preliminaries Preliminaries
          %p
            The axioms of a Yoneda structure are out to capture the properties of the presheaf construction with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CAT"} CAT
            replaced by general 2-category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi 𝒦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\mathcal{K}|
            as well as a class of 1-cells that behave well with respect to this class and the presheaf construction. In fact, it suffices to describe the admissible 1-cells since one can then identify the admissible 0-cells with the admissible identity 1-cells.
          %p
            In
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CAT
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CAT
            relative to the usual presheaf construction one should think of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small categories
            as the admissible 0-cells i.e. those categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            with all Hom-sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}(x,y)
            contained in a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            of “small” sets itself contained as object in a larger
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universe"} Grothendieck universe
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{B}
            are those with all relative Hom-sets
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}(f(a),b)\in Set
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "FS95"} Freyd-Street 1995
            %em a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi CAT
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}\in CAT
            %em is small
            i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\mathcal{C}|\in Set
            precisely if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\mathcal{C}^{op}}
            = succeed "." do
              %em are locally small
          %p
            Admissible functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            in this sense in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CAT
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CAT
            are closed under precomposition not only among themselves but with respect to arbitrary (composable)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            since the relative Hom-sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}(f{}g(x),b)
            are simply a subclass of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}(f(a),b)
            namely those for which
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi im
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in im(g)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+ideal"} right ideal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KZ+doctrines"} KZ doctrines
            and Yoneda structures it will nevertheless be useful to consider the more general case of closure in itself under composition as well, a situation which we acknowledge terminologically with the prefix “proto”.
          #admissible_1cell.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔸
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}
              be a class of 1-cells. The 1-cells
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝔸
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\in \mathbb{A}
              (and by abuse, the class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔸
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}
              as well) are called
              %em admissible
              if for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝔸
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\in \mathbb{A}
              and composable 1-cells
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\in \mathcal{K}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝔸
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ g\in \mathbb{A}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝔸
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\in \mathbb{A}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔸
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}
              are called
              %em proto-admissible
              if this closure property holds for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝔸
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\in \mathbb{A}
          #admissible_0cell.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔸
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}
              be an admissible (resp. proto-admissible) class of 1-cells. A 0-cell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi 𝒦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\in |\mathcal{K}|
              is called
              %em admissible
              (resp.
              = succeed ")" do
                %em proto-admissible
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_C
              is admissible (resp. proto-admissible). We denote the corresponding class of 0-cells by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\mathbb{A}|
          %p
            For admissible
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔸
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi 𝔸
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\in|\mathbb{A}|
            iff all 1-cells with codomain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            are admissible. This formulation has the advantage that it makes sense for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-category"} semi-categories
            as well.
          %p
            Having now “taken care” of the size issues we recall/introduce some terminology concerning
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extensions"} Kan extensions
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+adjoint+functor"} relative adjoint functors
            that will prove effective in yielding a surprisingly concise axiomatic description of the presheaf construction.
          #left_extension.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo :
                    %mi f
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi e
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta:f\Rightarrow e\circ g
              be a 2-cell:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mi g
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ⇓
                              %mi η
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↗
                              %mi e
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    A&amp; &amp; \overset{f}{\longrightarrow} &amp; &amp;B
                    \\\
                    \&amp;{}_g\searrow &amp; \Downarrow _\eta&amp; \nearrow _e&amp;
                    \\\
                    \&amp; &amp; C &amp; &amp;
                    }
            %p
              We say that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
              (or, by abuse, the diagram) exhibits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e:C\to B
              as a
              %em left extension
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to B
              %em along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:A\to C
              if for all parallel maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k:C\to B
              pasting with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
              induces a bijection between 2-cells
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo :
                    %mi e
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma:e\Rightarrow k
              and 2-cells
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\Rightarrow k\circ g
            %p
              We say that a 1-cell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:B\to D
              %em preserves
              this left extension if the following diagram exhibits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\circ e
              as a left extension of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\circ f
              along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              \:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi D
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mrow
                                %mi h
                                %mrow
                                %mi f
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ⇘
                              %mi id
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ↖
                              %mi h
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mi g
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ⇓
                              %mi η
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↗
                              %mi e
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{ &amp;     &amp;D    &amp; &amp;
                    \\\
                    
                    \&amp;{}^{h{}f}{\nearrow}&amp;\seArrow^{id} &amp;{\nwarrow}^h&amp;
                    \\\
                    A&amp; &amp; \overset{f}{\longrightarrow} &amp; &amp;B
                    \\\
                    \&amp;{}_g\searrow &amp; \Downarrow _\eta&amp; \nearrow _e&amp;
                    \\\
                    \&amp; &amp; C &amp; &amp;
                    }
            %p
              The left extension is called
              %em absolute
              if it is preserved by all 1-cells with domain
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          #left_lifting.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi f
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi l
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi:f\Rightarrow g\circ l
              be a 2-cell:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi l
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mi f
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ⇗
                              %mi ϕ
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↙
                              %mi g
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    
                    A&amp; &amp; \overset{l}{\longrightarrow} &amp; &amp;C
                    \\\
                    \&amp;{}_f\searrow &amp; \neArrow _\phi&amp; \swarrow _g&amp;
                    \\\
                    \&amp; &amp; B &amp; &amp;
                    }
            %p
              We say that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              (or, by abuse, the diagram) exhibits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi l
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l:A\to C
              as a
              %em left lifting
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to B
              %em through
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:C\to B
              iff for all parallel maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k:A\to C
              pasting with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              induces a bijection between 2-cells
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo :
                    %mi l
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma:l\Rightarrow k
              and 2-cells
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\Rightarrow g\circ k
            %p
              We say that a 1-cell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j:D\to A
              preserves this left lifting if the following diagram exhibits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi l
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l\circ j
              as a left lifting of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ j
