          %h2#extension_KZ-doctrine The relation to KZ doctrines
          %p
            Whereas it was shown already in the 1970s that ordinary monads on a 1-category can be brought into an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+system"} extension form
            that avoids the iteration of the endofunctor similar presentations for 2-dimensional monad theory evolved more recently. For the comparison of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax-idempotent+2-monad"} lax-idempotent 2-monads
            aka
            %em KZ doctrines
            to Yoneda structures such presentations provided in the work of Marmolejo and Wood (
            %a{:href => "#MW12"}> 2012
            ) come in handy. The link to Yoneda structures has been made in Walker (
            %a{:href => "#Walker17"}> 2017
            ).
          #KZ_doctrine.num_defn
            %h6#definition_7 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              %em KZ-doctrine (in extension form)
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              is a pair
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,y)
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              assigns to every 0-cell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\in\mathcal{K}
              a 0-cell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(A)\in\mathcal{K}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              is a family of 1-cells
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_A:A\to P(A)
              indexed by the 0-cells
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\in\mathcal{K}
              such that
            %ul
              %li
                For every pair of 0-cells
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                and 1-cells
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to P(B)
                there exists an invertible 2-cell
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_f
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi P
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi B
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi y
                                  %mi A
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ⇓
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi ϵ
                                  %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↗
                              %mover
                                %mi f
                                %mo ¯
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi P
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi A
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    A&amp; &amp; \overset{f}{\longrightarrow} &amp; &amp;P(B)
                    \\\
                    \&amp;{}_{y_A}\searrow &amp; \Downarrow _{\epsilon_f}&amp; \nearrow _\overline{f}&amp;
                    \\\
                    \&amp; &amp; P(A) &amp; &amp;
                    }
            %p
              that exhibits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{f}
              as left extension of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              along
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B=A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f=y_A
              then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi y
                          %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_{y_A}
              is given by the identity 2-cell
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi id
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi y
                            %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mi A
                      %mo ⇒
                      %msub
                        %mi id
                        %mrow
                          %mi P
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_{y_A}:y_A\Rightarrow id_{P(A)}\circ y_A
            %ul
              %li
                For any 1-cell
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo :
                        %mi B
                        %mo →
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:B\to P(C)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi g
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo :
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{g}:P(B)\to P(C)
                given itself by the left extension along
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_B
                preserves the left extension of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to B
                along
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_A
                exhibited by
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϵ
                          %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_f
          %p
            Clearly, with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,y)
            the presheaf construction of a Yoneda structure comes into sight though we still need to define a suitable class of admissible maps from
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,y)
          %p
            But before we do this we will introduce the concept that corresponds to the familiar notion of a pseudoalgebra for a lax-idempotent 2-monad thereby hopefully making it plausible that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,y)
            indeed is equivalent to the usual algebraic concept.
          %p
            The main idea of the following definition is that the “pseudoalgebras”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi 𝒦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in|\mathcal{K}|
            mimic the extension properties of the
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(A)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(A)
            satisfy the condition trivially and should be thought of as free algebras.
          #P-complete.num_defn
            %h6#definition_8 Definition
            %p
              Given a KZ-doctrine
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,y)
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi 𝒦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in|\mathcal{K}|
              is called
              %em P-cocomplete
              if for every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:B\to X
              there exists an invertible 2-cell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_g
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi g
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi y
                                  %mi B
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ⇓
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi ϵ
                                  %mi g
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↗
                              %mover
                                %mi g
                                %mo ¯
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi P
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi B
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    B&amp; &amp; \overset{g}{\longrightarrow} &amp; &amp;X
                    \\\
                    \&amp;{}_{y_B}\searrow &amp; \Downarrow _{\epsilon_g}&amp; \nearrow _\overline{g}&amp;
                    \\\
                    \&amp; &amp; P(B) &amp; &amp;
                    }
            %p
              that exhibits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi g
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{g}
              as left extension of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              along
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi g
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{g}:P(B)\to X
              preserves all the left extensions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{f}:P(A)\to P(B)
              along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_A
              of arbitrary
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to P(B)
          #P-cell.num_defn
            %h6#definition_9 Definition
            %p
              A 1-cell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:X\to Y
              between two P-cocomplete objects
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is called a
              %em P-homomorphism
              (, or a
              = succeed ")" do
                %em P-cell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
              preserves the left extension
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{f}:P(A)\to X
              along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_A:A\to P(A)
              for every
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to X
          #P-algebra.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Given a KZ-doctrine
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,y)
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ∈
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi 𝒦
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\in|\mathcal{K}|
                  is P-cocomplete;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi y
                          %mi A
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_A:A\to P(A)
                  has a left adjoint with invertible counit;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                  is the underlying object of a pseudoalgebra.
          %p
            %strong> Proof
            \. A combination of results from Bunge-Funk (
            %a{:href => "#BF99"}> 1999
            ) and Marmolejo-Wood (
            %a{:href => "#MW12"}> 2012
            ).
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %p
            We now attend the problem of defining a class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔸
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}
            of admissible maps for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,y)
          #P-admissible.num_defn
            %h6#definition_10 Definition
            %p
              Given a KZ-doctrine
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,y)
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:B\to C
              is called
              %em P-admissible
              if the left extension of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi B
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_B:B\to P(B)
              along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              exists and moreover is preserved by all left extensions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi h
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{h}:P(B)\to X
              along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_B
              of 1-cells
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:B\to X
              into a P-cocomplete 0-cell
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            A crucial property of the Yoneda embedding is of course that is in fact an
            %em embedding
            whence we must demand the same for the units of a KZ-doctrine:
          #locally_fully_faithful.num_defn
            %h6#definition_11 Definition
            %p
              A KZ-doctrine
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,y)
              on a 2-category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              is called
              %em locally fully-faithful
              if all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_A: A\to P(A)
              are representably fully-faithful (cf.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#fully_faithful"} ex.
          %p Now we are ready to state the main result of this section.
          %p
          #Yoneda_monad.num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,y)
              be a locally fully-faithful KZ-doctrine on 2-category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}_P
              the class of P-admissible 1-cells. The pair
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{A}_P,P)
              defines a proto-Yoneda structure on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
          (
          %a{:href => "#Walker17"}> Walker 2017 p.9
          )
          %p
            Jokingly, we may say that
            %em a general KZ-doctrine is nothing but unfaithful Yoneda-structure without size problems!
            Conversely, the main difference between a locally fully-faithful KZ-doctrine and a Yoneda structure concerns size: For the KZ-doctrine every identity morphism is admissible and, accordingly, its presheaf construction is total whereas this need not be the case for general Yoneda structures.
          %h2#in_retrospective In retrospective
          %p
            The following is a quote from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street's
            “Australian conspectus of higher categories” (
            %a{:href => "#Street10"}> Street 2010
            , p. 241):
          %blockquote
            %p
              In 1971 Bob Walters and I began work on Yoneda structures on 2-categories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a{:href => "#KS73"} KS1
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a{:href => "#SW78"} StW
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to \mathcal{P}A
              of a category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              into its presheaf category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}A =[ A^{op} ,Set]
              \. We covered the more general example of categories enriched in a base
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝒱
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}A = [A^{op} ,\mathcal {V}]
              \. Clearly size considerations needed to be taken seriously although a motivating size-free example was preordered sets with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}A
              the inclusion-ordered set of right order ideals in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a{:href => "#KS73"} KS1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              that an elementary topos was precisely a finitely complete category with a
              %em power object
              (that is, a relations classifier). This meant that my work with Walters could be viewed as a higher-dimensional version of topos theory. As usual when raising dimension, what we might mean by a 2-dimensional topos could be many things, several of which could be useful. I looked
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a{:href => "#Street74"} St6
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a{:href => "#Street80"} St8
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              at those special Yoneda structures where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}A
              classified two-sided discrete fibrations.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            %li
